# MESI cache implementation notes

## Scope and topology

- The demo contains two blocking, direct-mapped, write-back L1 caches over one
  ideal cache-line-wide LLC.
- A cache sends `coh_cache2bus_req` requests through the request-level
  round-robin arbiter. The arbiter supplies the requester ID to
  `cache_coherency_global`.
- The global controller broadcasts one accepted operation to every other cache
  through `coh_bus2cache_req`, waits for every snoop response, OR-reduces their
  pre-transition `shared` results, and replies to the requester.
- The global controller and arbiter serialize coherence operations. They do not
  wait for the requester to finish its later LLC fill. A cache that is still
  using a direct-mapped index backpressures an unsafe snoop for that index.

## Local CPU-side MESI decision

- The CPU address, data, and read/write operation are captured when the blocking
  upstream request is accepted.
- Hit and MESI state are evaluated continuously from that captured address and
  the current cache entry. Do not use the live upstream address after the
  request handshake.
- While a cache-to-bus request is unaccepted, its operation is a live decision.
  A snoop may change the entry and therefore change an outstanding `BusUpgr`
  into `BusRdX`. This custom blocking bus samples the operation only when
  `req_val && req_rdy` and permits the payload to change before acceptance.
- At cache-to-bus request acceptance, the bus operation and both candidate MESI
  results are frozen. The returned `shared` bit selects the final result.
- `BusNOP` is consumed inside `cache_coherency_local`; it never reaches the
  global bus. An accepted `BusNOP` locks its index through the local cache
  commit so a concurrent snoop cannot make the result stale.

## Cache metadata and allocation

- MESI is the only validity/dirty metadata. `I` means invalid and `M` means
  dirty; the cache line has no separate valid or dirty bits.
- A hit requires a tag match and a state other than `I`. A victim requires a
  writeback exactly when its state is `M`.
- Every miss performs a full-line LLC read after coherence completes. A write
  miss merges the requested 64-bit word into that returned line and installs it
  in `M`; it never zero-fills the untouched words.
- A normal modified eviction performs a full-line writeback and commits `M->I`
  before the main FSM proceeds to coherence lookup for the replacement.

## Snoop responder contract

- The local snoop responder is an independent blocking FSM. Its RTL body is
  intentionally left for the local-coherence implementation step.
- A snoop request is accepted only when its direct-mapped index is safe. Index
  comparison is derived by shifting away the cache-line offset; different tags
  mapping to the same index conflict.
- A snoop may be accepted while another cache-to-bus request from this cache is
  mutable and unaccepted. This prevents simultaneous requests from deadlocking
  and lets the local operation be re-evaluated.
- Once accepted, the snoop index remains reserved through the snoop response
  handshake. New upstream accesses to that index are backpressured.
- `rsp_shared` reports whether the cache held any valid copy before the snoop:
  `S`, `E`, or `M` all return one; `I` or a tag miss returns zero.
- `BusRd`: `E/S->S`; `M` writes the full line to LLC and then becomes `S`.
- `BusRdX`: `E/S->I`; `M` writes the full line to LLC and then becomes `I`.
- `BusUpgr`: a legal `S` copy becomes `I` without LLC traffic. Encountering
  `E` or `M` is a MESI invariant violation and should assert in simulation.
- `bus2cache.rsp_val` is asserted only after any required LLC writeback and the
  cache-state commit have both completed.

## Shared LLC manager

- Main and snoop FSMs submit one-entry, full-line LLC request bundles to a
  dedicated manager implemented with its own `always_comb` and `always_ff`.
- The manager's `COMMIT` state selects a pending coherence request before a
  pending main request. An already active AXI transaction is never preempted.
- Read ownership lasts from selection through the `AR` and `R` handshakes.
- Write ownership lasts through independent `AW` and `W` handshakes and the
  final `B` handshake.
- After AXI completion, the manager holds its owner and response stable until
  that producer consumes the response. Only then does it return to `COMMIT`.

## Cache commit path

- Main and snoop FSMs submit field-enabled cache updates through one selector.
  A coherence update has priority over a simultaneous main update.
- Every update writes MESI state. `tag_we` and `data_we` control replacement of
  the tag and full-line data; snoop state changes and eviction invalidation set
  both write-enables low.
- A losing producer holds its update until accepted. The cache array is written
  by only one sequential block.

## Required end-to-end stress case

- Fill one line into `S` in both caches, then issue simultaneous writes to two
  different words. The arbiter-selected first writer upgrades, the second
  request re-evaluates from its snoop-invalidated state and uses `BusRdX`, and
  the final cache line must contain both word updates.


